<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Onitsha</h1><p class="st-meta">1650 CE – 1900 CE (approx.) · capital Onitsha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Igbo kingdom on the east bank of the Niger, ruled by an Obi in a court style borrowed from Benin and later a gateway for missions and British trade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to tradition, Onitsha was founded in the 17th century by migrants from the west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, led by Eze Chima, who left the area of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a> after a dispute and crossed the river. They settled among existing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> communities on the east bank and created a kingdom unusual in a region where most Igbo lived in small, chiefless village groups.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Obi ruled with the Ndichie, a council of titled chiefs ranked in grades, and the ozo title system gave wealthy men a route into public life. Royal ceremony, such as the annual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ofala-festival">Ofala festival</a>, echoed the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>. The town lived from river trade in yams, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> and slaves. Anglican missionaries under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-ajayi-crowther">Samuel Ajayi Crowther</a> arrived in 1857.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 1870s British traders and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-niger-company-territories">Royal Niger Company</a> made Onitsha a commercial base, and a British gunboat shelled the town in 1879. It was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-southern-nigeria">Protectorate of Southern Nigeria</a> around 1900. The Obi survives as a traditional ruler and Onitsha became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria's</a> largest market towns.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1650 CE – 1900 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.54%;width:4.98%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1650 CE – 1900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Onitsha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Igbo traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Igbo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Onitsha</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1775&amp;focus=kingdom-of-onitsha" aria-label="Kingdom of Onitsha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4868 1948 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Onitsha lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5188 2204L5187 2202"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5187 2202h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5188 2204h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5188 2204h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Onitsha. <a href="/atlas?year=1775&amp;focus=kingdom-of-onitsha">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

